{"BlockedEmail.should_block? performs a database write (record.match_count += 1; record.save) inside the EmailValidator#validate_each callback, violating Rails conventions that validations should be side-effect-free and causing double-counting since user.valid? and user.save both trigger validations in the controller": 0.9230517745018005, "CreateAccountController caches any server-side email error into rejectedEmails and always reports it as user.email.invalid, so emails rejected for other reasons (e.g., already-taken, domain-not-allowed, blocked) become incorrectly treated as syntactically invalid in the modal": 0.8913520574569702, "BlockedEmail.should_block? does a case-sensitive exact match on the raw email string, so a blocked entry can be bypassed by changing casing/normalization because User emails are not downcased before validation": 0.902585506439209}